{"reasoning": "The original question asks why 'Common Sense' was an important work, specifically noting that it shares an author with 'Rights of Man'.\n\nStep 1: Identify the author. Passage 1 and Passage 2 confirm Thomas Paine authored both 'Common Sense' (1776) and 'Rights of Man' (1791). This fulfills the premise of the question.\n\nStep 2: Determine the importance of 'Common Sense'. The documents provide several reasons for its importance:\n- It was an immediate success, quickly spreading 100,000 copies in three months to the two million residents of the 13 colonies. (Passage 1)\n- It crystallized sentiment for independence in 1776 and is credited with being 'the most widely read pamphlet of the American Revolution.' (Passage 1)\n- It provided a new and convincing argument for independence by advocating a complete break with history, compelling readers to make an immediate choice. (Passage 1)\n- It pioneered a style of political writing suited to the democratic society he envisioned, rendering complex ideas intelligible to average readers. (Passage 1)\n- Sophia Rosenfeld argues Paine was innovative in using 'common sense' as a weapon to de-legitimize the monarchy and overturn conventional wisdom.\n- Robert Middlekauff states it became immensely popular because Paine appealed to widespread convictions that monarchy was preposterous.\n\nThe documents strongly establish the importance of 'Common Sense' based on its popularity, persuasive power in advocating independence from Britain, and its innovative style.", "finding": "Thomas Paine authored both Common Sense (1776) and Rights of Man (1791). Common Sense crystallized sentiment for independence in 1776, became the most widely read pamphlet of the American Revolution, encouraged recruitment, provided a new argument for independence by advocating a complete break with history, and pioneered a clear, concise style suitable for democratic readers. It also employed 'common sense' as a weapon to de-legitimize monarchy.", "answer": "It crystallized sentiment for independence in 1776, became the most widely read pamphlet of the American Revolution, provided a new and convincing argument for independence by advocating a complete break with history, and pioneered a style of political writing suited to the democratic society he envisioned.", "next_query": null}
